  "descriptionShort": "Annual percent change in gross domestic product. This data is adjusted for inflation.",
  "descriptionFromProducer": "Gross domestic product, constant prices. Percent change. Annual percentages of constant price GDP are year-on-year changes; the base year is country-specific. Expenditure-based GDP is total final expenditures at purchasers' prices (including the f.o.b. value of exports of goods and services), less the f.o.b. value of imports of goods and services. [SNA 1993]",
  "descriptionProcessing": "For each country, the IMF reports the last year for which it has actual data. We show the years up to and including that year as observations, and the later years as projections. For some countries, these later years include recent past years for which the IMF has only its own estimates, because official data isn’t available yet.\n\nSome countries report their data by fiscal year. The IMF shows each fiscal year under one calendar year: for example, India’s fiscal year from April 2025 to March 2026 appears as 2025, and Egypt’s fiscal year from July 2024 to June 2025 also appears as 2025. We date the last year of actual data in the same way.\n\nThe IMF doesn’t report a last year of actual data for most of its country groups, such as the world or advanced economies. For these, as in the IMF’s own World Economic Outlook tables, the year of the release and later years are projections.\n\nWe don’t show GDP growth for Laos in 2024. The IMF’s figures for Laos come from its own estimates up to 2023 and from the Lao authorities from 2024 onward, so the published growth rate for 2024 (13.5%) compares two different sources rather than measuring real growth. The IMF has confirmed this break in its data.",
