  "descriptionKey": "- [Gross domestic product](#dod:gdp) (GDP) is a comprehensive measure of a country’s total income. It helps compare the size of economies and track how they change over time. It is especially useful for understanding trends in economic growth.\n- The GDP is calculated as the value of all final goods and services produced each year in a country. It represents the total economic output of a country or region.\n- Growth is the percent change in GDP from one year to the next, after adjusting for inflation: if a country’s inflation-adjusted (real) GDP is 2% higher than the year before, growth is 2%. Growth for the world and other country groups is an average of each country’s growth, weighted by the size of its economy measured at [purchasing power parity](#dod:purchasing-power-parity) (PPP).\n- The International Monetary Fund (IMF) makes its projections country by country, and then combines them into regional and global figures. All of them rest on the same assumptions about exchange rates, oil prices, and interest rates, and assume that each country’s current policies continue. They are the IMF’s best estimates, not firm predictions: the IMF revises them in every World Economic Outlook, published in April and in September or October, and they can change a lot when new data comes in or unexpected events hit.",
